# METHOD COVERAGE STATUS -- the only place a cell of qa/method-inventory.json may be declared
# anything other than MISSING.
#
# Owner's ruling, 2026-08-12: "i want a customer to be able to do anything mcp says it can do and
# anything a2a says it can do. so if thats calling 20 methods we need to support it. how internally
# though doesnt matter. i want full alphabet coverage not just A-J. I want A-Z"
#
# Owner's ruling, 2026-08-14, which is why the waived section below is EMPTY and stays that way:
# "100%, and the waiver list should stay empty." A recorded IMPOSSIBILITY -- a cell that cannot be
# built without defecting from the specification, the conformance suite or a security property --
# lives in qa/WAIVERS.md with a one-paragraph argument, enforced by the same gate that reads this
# file. Anything less than impossible is either implemented here or owed in the missing file.
#
# THREE STATES PER CELL in THIS file, and there is no fourth:
#
#   implemented                          the cell works, and an instrument was watched saying so
#   waived <YYYY-MM-DD> <reason>         deliberately absent, with a reason a customer could read
#                                        (empty by the ruling above; impossibilities go to
#                                        qa/WAIVERS.md, product decisions go back to the queue)
#   (absent from this file)              MISSING -- and MISSING FAILS THE BUILD, unless the cell is
#                                        a recorded impossibility in qa/WAIVERS.md
#
# FORMAT, following crates/busbar/src/config/config-schema.waivers, which is the house waiver
# format and is this file's precedent:
#
#   - One entry per line:  <exact-cell-id> = implemented
#                          <exact-cell-id> = waived <YYYY-MM-DD> <reason>
#   - EXACT cell ids only. No globs. `*` or `?` is a hard error -- a wildcard waiver is how a
#     whole transport disappears in one line.
#   - A reason is MANDATORY on a waiver. `= waived 2026-08-12` with no text is a hard error.
#   - A STALE entry (a cell id that is not in qa/method-inventory.json) is a hard error, so a
#     method that the SDKs renamed cannot keep an entry that no longer excuses anything.
#   - Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A CONSTANT IN THE TEST: a waiver here is a line in the PR diff, sitting next
# to the obligation it excuses, dated, with the reasoning attached. That is the whole point.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS A LEGITIMATE WAIVER. The PUSH-DELIVER precedent (goal D16): PUSH-DELIVER-001/002/
# 003 stay red on purpose because the TCK's webhook receiver defaults to localhost and busbar's
# SSRF guard correctly refuses it. A security control winning over a conformance score is a
# legitimate waiver. "We ran out of time" is ALSO a legitimate waiver -- but only if it is written
# down here, dated, with the customer-visible consequence spelled out.
#
# WHY THIS FILE STARTED EMPTY OF `implemented` LINES, and why it no longer is.
# `crates/busbar/src/mcp/` and `crates/busbar/src/a2a/` are being deleted whole (goal D10) and
# rebuilt cell by cell on the Protocol x Operation x Transport axes. Claiming a cell here on the
# strength of code that is scheduled for deletion would be claiming coverage the release does not
# have, so every cell began MISSING, the full list is pinned in qa/method-coverage.missing, and
# THAT LIST IS THE WORK QUEUE FOR THE REST OF 1.6.0. A cell is claimed here when its leg has been
# watched green on an instrument -- read the output, not the tick. The first cells to leave the
# queue are the A2A plane's HTTP+JSON and gRPC server sides, below, and each one names the
# requirement the publisher's own suite passed on that leg's parameterisation.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# implemented
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GetExtendedAgentCard / agent/getAuthenticatedExtendedCard, busbar as SERVER, over JSON-RPC.
#
# WHAT BUSBAR PUBLISHES, because for a gateway this is a design decision before it is a method.
# The extended card is the AUTHENTICATED half of a two-tier split: the public card at the
# well-known path is unauthenticated, so it enumerates no fronted agent at all, and this one names
# exactly the agents THIS CALLER may invoke -- selected by `catalogue::inbound_catalogue`, which is
# the same judgement that decides dispatch. So the card can never name an agent a submission would
# refuse, and can never show one tenant another tenant's inventory. The naive implementation --
# union every fronted agent's skills -- is a data-exposure bug rather than a conformance one, and
# it is the reason this cell was called out as a decision rather than as unbuilt work.
#
# One skill per AGENT, identified by busbar's own agent id, and not the backends' own skills
# merged: skill ids are upstream-authored and namespaced by nothing, so two vendors both declaring
# `summarise` produce one id twice -- and `card::skill_digests` REFUSES a card with a duplicate
# skill id, so a peer applying to busbar the rule busbar applies to everyone else would reject
# busbar's own card outright.
#
# INSTRUMENT. `scripts/a2a-subject/boot.sh` drives the verb over the real wire with a real
# audience-bound credential, in BOTH live spellings of the name, and asserts the entitled agent is
# named and the backend authority is not. That is the instrument because the official suite cannot
# be one here: CORE-CAP-003 only runs when the capability is ABSENT, and CARD-EXT-002 skips itself
# the moment the card is configured, so a busbar that answers this perfectly and one that answers
# it with garbage produce identical TCK output.
a2a|jsonrpc|server|client|GetExtendedAgentCard = implemented

# PushNotificationDelivery, busbar as SERVER, over HTTP+JSON -- the outbound POST to a caller's
# webhook. (The JSON-RPC and gRPC columns are N/A with a reason: there is no delivery rpc.)
#
# Two things were owed and missing, and both are built. The config's `authentication` was DROPPED
# at registration -- the durable row held one URL string, the verbs echoed `{id, url}`, and the
# delivery sent `content-type` and nothing else -- so a customer whose webhook authenticates could
# not use push notifications at all. It is now stored and presented as `<scheme> <credentials>` on
# the `Authorization` header. And the delivered body was a BARE Task where the protocol defines a
# `StreamResponse`; the specification's own validator rejected every busbar delivery, and the same
# document nested under `"task"` validates.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `a2a/tests/pushdeliver_tests.rs` (the header, the envelope, the withdrawal, and the
# secret not outliving the task) and `a2a/tests/relay_stream_tests.rs`, which drives a real task to
# completion and reads what went on the wire.
#
# THE THREE TCK MUSTs STAY RED, and that is recorded rather than laundered.
# PUSH-DELIVER-001/002/003 fail because the suite's own webhook receiver is plaintext `http://` by
# literal and busbar refuses a plaintext callback before it looks at the address -- the owner's
# ruling, recorded in testing/a2a-tck/WAIVERS.md. This cell is claimed on the capability, which is
# owed to every customer whose webhook is https, and NOT on those three going green. Conformance
# being unobservable here is not permission to leave the capability unbuilt, and it is not
# permission to claim the three either.
a2a|http+json|server|server|PushNotificationDelivery = implemented

# ── AND THE ROWS THIS FILE LOST IN A MERGE, RESTORED. ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The three blocks above and the one below arrived on two branches. The merge that brought them
# together kept every PROSE block and dropped twelve of the ROWS they were written about, so
# `pinned_missing_set_is_exact` read twelve cells as having regressed into MISSING while their
# evidence sat right here. The rows are restored verbatim from the commit that earned them
# (`c63c966a`, `bba2709a`); nothing new is claimed.

# A2A's HTTP+JSON BINDING, SERVER SIDE. busbar receives these over `POST /a2a/message:send`,
# `POST /a2a/message:stream`, `GET /a2a/tasks`, `GET /a2a/tasks/{id}`, `POST /a2a/tasks/{id}:cancel`,
# `POST /a2a/tasks/{id}:subscribe` and the `pushNotificationConfigs` collection, and the card busbar
# serves advertises the `HTTP+JSON` interface beside `JSONRPC`.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT IS THE PUBLISHER'S OWN SUITE, watched, not inferred. `scripts/a2a-subject/boot.sh
# --tck` boots a busbar built from this commit and runs a2aproject/a2a-tck at its pinned ref against
# it; the `http_json` transport row went from `3/83 (80 skipped)` to `84/96 (6 skipped)` and every
# one of these operations has a named requirement passing on the `[http_json]` parameterisation:
#
#   SendMessage / SendStreamingMessage  CORE-HIST-001..004, CORE-STREAM-001..003
#   GetTask                             CORE-HIST-002, CORE-HIST-004
#   ListTasks                           CORE-LIST-001..005
#   CancelTask                          CORE-CANCEL-001..003
#   SubscribeToTask                     STREAM-SUB-002..004, STREAM-ORDER-001..004
#   Create/Get/List/DeleteTaskPushNotificationConfig
#                                       PUSH-CREATE-001/002, PUSH-GET-001/002, PUSH-LIST-001,
#                                       PUSH-DEL-001/002
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED HERE, though its route exists:
#   * `GetExtendedAgentCard` -- not claimed HERE, and the reason moved: this unit armed the routes,
#     and the verb behind them landed separately. The JSON-RPC cell above is the one an instrument
#     watched; the HTTP+JSON cell stays in the queue until one watches it there.
#   * `PushNotificationDelivery` -- PUSH-DELIVER-001/002/003 still FAIL on BOTH bindings, for the
#     documented SSRF reason. Arming a second binding does not change a security decision.
#   * every `client` role cell -- busbar speaks JSON-RPC to the backends it fronts. This unit armed
#     the SERVER side of the binding and nothing else.
a2a|http+json|server|client|CancelTask = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|CreateTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|DeleteTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|GetTask = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|GetTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|ListTaskPushNotificationConfigs = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|ListTasks = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|SendMessage = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|SendStreamingMessage = implemented
a2a|http+json|server|client|SubscribeToTask = implemented

# tools/call, busbar as CLIENT, over streamable-HTTP -- the outbound call to a registered upstream.
# `obligation: issue`: busbar originates this one, so the evidence has to be what busbar PUT ON THE
# WIRE, not what it accepted.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT, AND WHY IT IS A NEW ONE. `testing/mcp-conformance/src/suites/client-role.mjs` has
# fourteen CLI.* scenarios and every one of them skipped whenever `ctx.target.hasClientRole` was
# false, which `src/core/target.mjs` derives from whether a `clientLaunch` was configured. Nothing
# configured one, so THOSE FOURTEEN HAD NEVER RUN AGAINST BUSBAR -- while `187 passed, 0 failed`
# read as a verdict on "busbar implements MCP". It was a verdict on the SERVER role and silent about
# the other half, which is the same shape of false green as a disarmed leg. The leg is armed in
# `scripts/mcp-conformance.sh` by `scripts/mcp-subject/client-arm.sh`, against a busbar booted from
# THIS COMMIT -- never a deployment, for the settled reason that a gate depending on one produces a
# green meaning "the deployment was fine yesterday" and a red meaning "somebody redeployed".
#
# WHAT THE INSTRUMENT SHOWED, read off the fake server's own transcript of busbar's bytes:
#
#   client.methodsUsed        ["tools/call"]        -- the one method busbar issues, see below
#   client.protocolVersions   ["2026-07-28"]        -- on every request, in params._meta
#   client.declaredCapabilities []                  -- the honest empty set: busbar answers nothing
#   client.idType             "number", reusedIds [] -- non-null, unique while in flight
#
# and twelve of the thirteen push/pr scenarios PASS, plus CLI.HOSTILE.GIANT-RESULT at prerelease.
# The nine hostile ones are the load-bearing half: against a peer that opens an unsolicited
# server-initiated request, closes stdout mid-frame, never answers at all, answers with an id nobody
# asked for, asks for a password in a form-mode elicitation, asks for a capability busbar never
# declared, swaps a schema between discovery and invocation, lies about its own outputSchema, or
# advertises a `javascript:` icon URI, busbar answers none of it, leaks none of it, and gives up on
# its own deadline rather than hanging.
#
# THE ONE THAT IS RED IS NOT THIS CELL, AND IT IS NOT LAUNDERED HERE.
# `CLI.CACHE.TOLERATES-ABSENT-HINTS` fails, by name, because it needs the client to have called
# `tools/list` and busbar did not call it inside any window this rig can drive: busbar's outbound
# `tools/list` lives on the trust-refresh path (`mcp/connect.rs`), fired by a scheduled tick or an
# operator verb, not by the front-door dispatch the driver triggers. THIS instrument therefore says
# nothing about the client-side `tools/list` cell. It was NOT moved to a waiver to make a number go
# green, and the rig was NOT taught to poke the admin refresh verb to manufacture the request -- a
# driver arranged to produce the observation the test wants is not an observation. That cell is
# claimed further down, on the instrument that DOES drive the trust-refresh path
# (`connect_tests.rs`, `drift_dispatch_tests.rs`), and the two statements are consistent: one rig
# cannot see it, another one can.
#
# THE CELL ITSELF IS CLAIMED ONCE, in the client-role block below, beside `tools/list`. A second
# claim here is what `pinned_missing_set_is_exact` calls "claimed twice", and it was one: two
# branches recorded the same cell on two instruments and both rows survived the merge that brought
# them together. The evidence above is kept, because it is real and it is about this cell; only the
# duplicate ROW is gone.

# AND ONE THING THAT LANDED WITHOUT CLAIMING A CELL, RECORDED SO THAT ITS ABSENCE HERE IS NOT READ
# AS AN OVERSIGHT. `resources/subscribe`, `resources/unsubscribe`,
# `notifications/tools/list_changed` and `notifications/resources/updated` now exist as real code on
# the protocol x operation matrix, on the specification authors' own crate's types. That is the
# CODEC: it reads those bytes and it writes them, in either direction, and its tests are the wire
# contract. It is NOT the capability, and the difference is the whole reason a cell is claimed on an
# instrument rather than on a diff:
#
#   * the served MCP endpoint still answers those four names -32601, because the deployed surface is
#     stateless -- no session, no server-to-client stream -- and a subscription with no channel to be
#     notified over is a promise that would never be kept. Advertising it would be worse for a
#     customer than refusing it, because a client would wait rather than fall back;
#   * so a customer cannot yet do this, and a cell says a customer can.
#
# The cell was therefore MISSING and its line stayed in qa/method-coverage.missing, which was the
# correct state. What was left was the channel and the admission, not the vocabulary.
#
# ALL FOUR OF THOSE NAMES ARE NOW DECIDED, and the note above is kept rather than deleted because
# what it describes -- a codec that reads and writes the right bytes over an endpoint that answers
# -32601 -- is the exact shape a reader has to be able to recognise, and the shape this file exists
# to stop being mistaken for coverage. The four went four different ways, which is itself the point:
#
#   * `resources/subscribe` and `resources/unsubscribe` are RECORDED IMPOSSIBILITIES, argued in
#     qa/WAIVERS.md: the revision removed them and the official suite asserts they answer -32601,
#     so serving them is the defect;
#   * `notifications/tools/list_changed` is IMPLEMENTED, because the channel it needed --
#     `subscriptions/listen` -- is now served and the notification reaches a client over it;
#   * `notifications/resources/updated` is OWED WORK, pinned in qa/method-coverage.missing. Its old
#     waiver argued a capability narrowing, and per the owner's 2026-08-14 ruling a narrowing is a
#     decision, not an impossibility -- the client leg already handles an upstream's
#     `notifications/resources/updated`, so an upstream-events relay is design work, not magic.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MCP, busbar as SERVER, over STREAMABLE-HTTP — the whole column, decided cell by cell.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# THE ONE FACT THAT DECIDES HALF OF THIS COLUMN, and it is not a matter of taste: revision
# `2026-07-28` (SEP-2575) is STATELESS. It deleted the `initialize` handshake, protocol sessions,
# the standalone GET stream and `Last-Event-ID` resumability, and it deleted the methods that only
# meant anything inside a session. The official suite does not merely tolerate their absence — it
# REQUIRES it. `server-stateless` posts `initialize`, `ping`, `logging/setLevel`,
# `resources/subscribe` and `resources/unsubscribe` and asserts each answers HTTP 404 with JSON-RPC
# `-32601`, and `logging-set-level` and `ping` are scenarios marked `removedIn: 2026-07-28` and are
# not run at this revision at all.
#
# So for those methods, on this transport, IMPLEMENTING THEM IS THE DEFECT. A busbar that answered
# `initialize` would fail the conformance gate for doing it. They are waived below with that as the
# reason, because "we chose not to build it" and "the specification removed it and the referee
# checks that we did not build it" are different facts and only one of them is a gap.
#
# WHAT REPLACED THE CHANNEL, because "no GET stream" is not "no server-to-client messages" and
# reading it that way is what left four notification names in the tree as a codec with nothing to
# carry them. The revision MOVED the channel from a transport verb onto a method:
# `subscriptions/listen` is an ordinary POST whose response is a long-lived stream of notifications.
# That method is now built (`crates/busbar/src/mcp/subscribe.rs`), and it is what makes the
# list-changed notifications below claimable rather than merely spellable.
#
# AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE SERVER-ORIGINATED SURFACE travels a second way that is also not a
# stream: `elicitation/create`, `sampling/createMessage` and `roots/list` are asked by RETURNING an
# `InputRequiredResult` whose `inputRequests` name them, which the caller answers by re-issuing the
# request with `inputResponses`. busbar composes those from OPERATOR configuration alone
# (`ask_caller:`), filters them by the capabilities the caller declared, and seals them with a
# `requestState` busbar mints — see `crates/busbar/src/mcp/callerask.rs` for why an upstream's ask
# can never become one of these.

# ── THE CATALOGUE AND DISPATCH READS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# INSTRUMENT for all twelve: the official conformance suite, `--official-subject`, against a busbar
# built from this commit and booted on loopback by `scripts/mcp-subject/boot.sh` with a real
# audience-bound credential. Every one of these is the subject of at least one REQUIRED scenario of
# revision `2026-07-28` — `tools-list`, the seven `tools-call-*`, `prompts-list`, the four
# `prompts-get-*`, `resources-list`, `resources-read-text`, `resources-read-binary`,
# `resources-templates-read`, `sep-2164-resource-not-found`, `completion-complete`,
# `server-stateless` — and the leg is ARMED-OR-RED with set equality on the required scenario set,
# so a run that quietly exercised fewer of them is a hard failure rather than a smaller number.
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|server/discover = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|tools/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|tools/call = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|prompts/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|prompts/get = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|resources/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|resources/read = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|resources/templates/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|completion/complete = implemented

# SEP-2663, the tasks extension: `tools/call` answered with a task, then polled and steered. THREE
# methods and only three — `tasks/result` and `tasks/list` were REMOVED by the extension's v2 wire,
# so their absence is what makes them answer `-32601`, which is conformant rather than a gap.
# Advertised under `capabilities.extensions`, gated on the caller's own declaration (`-32021`, not
# `-32601`: the surface exists, this caller has not asked for it).
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|tasks/get = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|tasks/update = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|tasks/cancel = implemented

# ── THE SERVER-TO-CLIENT CHANNEL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `subscriptions/listen`, and the four notifications that make it worth opening.
#
# WHAT THE VOCABULARY ALONE DOES NOT BUY. The wire types for these notifications existed while the
# served endpoint answered `-32601` to every subscription verb, and that combination is worth naming
# because it looks like coverage and is not: a subscription with no channel to be notified over is a
# promise that would never be kept, and a client told it is subscribed will wait rather than fall
# back. What makes these cells claimable is the CHANNEL and the ADMISSION, not the vocabulary.
#
# HOW IT WORKS, and the two properties that are security properties rather than features:
#
#   * THE ACKNOWLEDGEMENT IS A NARROWING. `notifications/subscriptions/acknowledged` carries the
#     ACCEPTED subset of the requested filter, not a receipt for it. A client that asked for
#     something busbar cannot deliver learns so on the first frame instead of waiting.
#   * A CHANGE IS SCOPED TO THE CALLER WHO IS WATCHING. The pin generation moving is the cheap gate;
#     what decides whether a stream is woken is a comparison of the catalogue THIS CALLER CAN SEE,
#     under the same grant predicate that scopes `tools/list`. A registration this caller may not
#     reach must not wake this caller's stream, because that leaks the existence of another tenant's
#     inventory through timing.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/subscribe_tests.rs`, every case driven over a real socket
# against a real router — the acknowledgement arriving first and carrying the subscription id, the
# narrowing, the refusal of a subscription that could deliver nothing, a REAL catalogue change made
# through `AppHandle::swap` (the same seam an admin apply and a config reload go through) waking an
# OPEN stream, and a stream filtered to prompts not leaking a tool notification. Plus the official
# suite's `server-stateless`, whose `ServerSendsSubscriptionAck`, `ServerTagsSubscriptionId` and
# `ServerHonorsNotificationFilter` checks judge exactly these three properties and, before this
# landed, could only skip themselves over a surface that answered `-32601`.
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|subscriptions/listen = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/subscriptions/acknowledged = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/tools/list_changed = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/prompts/list_changed = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/resources/list_changed = implemented

# ── `notifications/resources/updated`, EMITTED — the upstream-events relay (2026-08-15) ─────────
#
# The last queue item of the waivers-to-zero unit: "an upstream-events relay onto
# subscriptions/listen's narrowed category." The category (`resourceSubscriptions`) was narrowed
# away on the sentence "busbar fronts that upstream and is told nothing when a resource's contents
# change", and that sentence stopped being true the day the client leg learned to read a peer's
# `notifications/resources/updated` — busbar IS told, by the upstream's own announcement, with no
# polling imposed on anybody. The two halves now meet on one bounded structure
# (`mcp::client::pool::ResourceUpdates`): the HTTP and stdio client legs record `(server, uri)` —
# the ONE payload field an untrusted peer's message gets to carry, length-capped and believed
# nowhere — and every open listen stream reads it by its own cursor and relays.
#
# THREE GATES on every delivered event, each load-bearing: the subscriber ASKED for the uri (the
# accepted list, itself narrowed at open to what the caller can see, so an acknowledgement never
# confirms another tenant's inventory); the uri resolves under the subscriber's LIVE grant
# (re-derived per poll, so a narrowed grant bites mid-stream); and the resolved resource belongs
# to THE SERVER THAT ANNOUNCED it (an upstream cannot speak for another registration's inventory
# by spelling its uri). `server/discover` flips `resources.subscribe` to `true` in the same
# commit, because a declaration and a delivery must keep agreeing in both directions.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/subscribe_tests.rs` --
#   an_upstream_resource_update_is_relayed_to_a_subscribed_stream (over a real socket),
#   the_relay_filters_unasked_uris_and_refuses_another_servers_announcement,
#   a_recorded_update_is_not_delivered_to_a_caller_whose_grant_does_not_reach_it (with control),
#   an_unreachable_resource_subscription_is_narrowed_away_in_the_acknowledgement;
# the client-leg recording halves are judged at THEIR carriers:
#   http_peer_tests::only_the_catalogue_notifications_move_the_catalogue (exactly one of the nine
#   notifications records, and it records the registered server id, not a peer-chosen name) and
#   stdio_client_leg_tests::a_peer_signal_brings_one_refresh_forward_and_is_then_rate_limited.
# Watched red first: with the relay short-circuited the central case fails on "the upstream
# announced an update and the open subscription was not told".
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/resources/updated = implemented

# ── THE NOTIFICATIONS THAT RIDE A REQUEST'S OWN RESPONSE STREAM ────────────────────────────────
#
# Neither of these needs a subscription, and that is the revision's design rather than a shortcut:
# with no session there is nowhere for a logging LEVEL to live and nowhere for a standing stream to
# push over, so both became per-request. The level is named in the request's own `_meta` and the
# records ride the response stream of the request they describe.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/sse_tests.rs` over a real socket, and the official
# suite's `server-stateless` (`ServerNoLogWithoutLogLevel` asserts busbar emits NO
# `notifications/message` for a request that named no level — the filter observed working rather
# than asserted) and `tools-call-with-progress` (REQUIRED), which drives an upstream that reports
# progress and reads what reached the caller. The progress frames are mapped back to the CALLER's
# own token: busbar mints its own for the upstream leg, so relaying the upstream's spelling would be
# uncorrelatable and relaying busbar's would leak an internal identifier.
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/message = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|notifications/progress = implemented

# ── THE THREE ASKS BUSBAR MAKES OF ITS CALLER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `elicitation/create`, `roots/list` and `sampling/createMessage`, issued as `inputRequests` on an
# `InputRequiredResult`. Under a stateless revision that is HOW a server originates a request: there
# is no standing stream to push one over, so the ask travels as a result and the answer travels as a
# fresh request carrying `inputResponses` and the sealed `requestState`.
#
# `sampling/createMessage` IN THIS DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE AND IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS THE
# CLIENT-DIRECTION ONE, which is claimed further down on its own instrument (the 2026-08-15
# per-upstream-budget unit). Here busbar asks the CALLER's model to run a
# completion on the CALLER's budget, in busbar's own name, from text an OPERATOR wrote — absent
# unless the operator opted in, per capability, literal, and refused outright if filtering by the
# caller's declared capabilities would empty the round (an ask that a caller could strip by
# declaring no capabilities is a gate its own subject controls).
#
# INSTRUMENT: the official suite's `input-required-result-basic-elicitation`,
# `-basic-sampling`, `-basic-list-roots` and the nine other `input-required-result-*` scenarios, all
# REQUIRED at this revision and all driven against the `ask_caller:` registrations in
# `scripts/mcp-subject/boot.sh`; plus `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/callerask_tests.rs`.
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|elicitation/create = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|roots/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|server|server|sampling/createMessage = implemented

# ── `notifications/roots/list_changed`, RECEIVED (2026-08-14, waiver-list-to-zero unit) ─────────
#
# The old waiver said "the answer is never stale enough to need invalidating", and that sentence
# was one word too strong: inside a sealed `requestState`'s TTL window, a caller can redeem a roots
# answer it gave BEFORE its roots changed. The notification is now the thing that ends that window
# -- a per-principal roots epoch (`crate::mcp::roots`), bumped by the real ingress notification
# arm, sealed into roots-bearing state at mint and compared at redemption. Scoped twice, and both
# scopes are asserted: to the PRINCIPAL who announced (another tenant's announcement moves
# nothing) and to ROOTS-BEARING state (a chatty client cannot void its own unrelated
# confirmations).
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/roots_changed_tests.rs` --
#   roots_state_minted_before_a_roots_change_is_refused_after_it (with recovery: the restarted
#   exchange dispatches on the caller's CURRENT roots),
#   one_principals_roots_change_does_not_invalidate_anothers_state,
#   a_roots_change_leaves_an_exchange_without_a_roots_ask_standing,
#   the_same_name_with_an_id_is_a_request_and_moves_nothing.
# Watched red first: with the epoch comparison disabled the central case fails on "a roots answer
# the caller itself disavowed must not be redeemed".
mcp|streamable-http|server|client|notifications/roots/list_changed = implemented

# ── MCP, busbar as CLIENT, over STREAMABLE-HTTP — the legs that are built ──────────────────────
#
# The rest of this column is still MISSING and is still in the work queue; what is claimed here is
# only what a customer can do today. busbar's outbound MCP leg exists to FRONT a registered tool
# server, so `tools/call` and `tools/list` are the two verbs it issues, and progress is the one
# message it accepts back and relays.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/upstream_join_tests.rs` and `deputy_pair_tests.rs` drive
# a REAL fake MCP peer and a REAL fake RFC 8693 token endpoint and read every byte that reached
# them — including that the outbound credential was selected under the INBOUND caller's grant, which
# is the confused-deputy defence for this direction and is only provable with both directions
# mounted. `connect_tests.rs` and `drift_dispatch_tests.rs` drive the catalogue read. The in-house
# conformance battery's six `pr`-tier seam tests drive the same leg against a HOSTILE HTTP peer
# through the same front door, with `MCP_NO_SKIPS=1` so a skipped seam is red.
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|tools/call = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|tools/list = implemented

# ── `roots/list`, ASKED BY AN UPSTREAM AND ANSWERED (2026-08-14, waiver-list-to-zero unit) ──────
#
# The old waiver's own text named the gap: "There is no roots policy in 1.6.0 for an operator to
# declare one from." There is now: `tools.<server>.roots` -- per registration, `file://` only,
# vetted at boot, refused at boot if declared behind a closed `grants.roots` gate. A granted
# upstream ask (`InputRequiredResult` naming `roots/list`) is satisfied from that declaration and
# from nothing else -- never busbar's own container filesystem -- and the retry carries MRTR's own
# `inputResponses`/`requestState` continuation, with the `roots` client capability declared from
# the FIRST request exactly when the deployment can honour it. Deny-by-default did not move: no
# grant refuses naming the grant, a grant with no declaration refuses naming the key
# (`Unsatisfiable` vs `Ungranted`, two remedies), and the ask terminates at busbar in every arm.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/roots_satisfy_tests.rs`, judged at the RECEIVING end of
# a real loopback peer --
#   a_granted_roots_ask_is_answered_with_the_operators_declared_roots,
#   an_ungranted_roots_ask_is_still_refused_and_names_the_grant,
#   a_granted_ask_with_no_declaration_refuses_and_names_the_key,
#   the_roots_policy_is_refused_at_boot_when_it_cannot_mean_what_it_says.
# Watched red first: with the satisfier short-circuited to its old refusal the central case fails
# on "the satisfied exchange must complete".
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|roots/list = implemented

# ── `sampling/createMessage`, ASKED BY AN UPSTREAM AND ANSWERED (2026-08-15) ────────────────────
#
# The queue's own text named the block: "a per-upstream sampling budget an operator can cap." There
# is now: `tools.<server>.sampling` -- per registration, the roots policy's shape on the other
# grant, vetted at boot, refused at boot if declared behind a closed `grants.sampling` gate or with
# a zero on either ceiling. Its three required fields are the three axes of the spend: `model` (the
# OPERATOR'S pool, never the ask's `modelPreferences` -- the payload is attacker-controlled and must
# not pick which provider to spend on), `max_tokens` (the ask's own number is clamped to it, which
# the protocol permits), and `max_requests_per_minute` (the per-upstream budget, spent BEFORE the
# model leg and carried across config applies on App, so an apply cannot refill a runaway).
#
# A granted, declared ask is satisfied by ONE governed completion PER MAP ENTRY through
# `ingress::operation_resolved` -- the SAME resolved core every arriving chat request enters, under
# the INBOUND caller's own key, budget, pool ACL and hooks; there is no thinner side channel, and
# the pool-ACL half is asserted (a caller whose key does not reach the declared pool is refused at
# admission with the provider untouched). The retry carries the protocol's `CreateMessageResult`
# under MRTR's `inputResponses`/`requestState` continuation, with the `sampling` client capability
# declared from the FIRST request exactly when the deployment can honour it. Deny-by-default did
# not move: no grant refuses naming the grant, a grant with no policy refuses naming the key, the
# budget past its cap refuses naming the budget key, and the ask terminates at busbar in every arm.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `crates/busbar/src/mcp/tests/sampling_satisfy_tests.rs`, judged at the RECEIVING end
# of a real loopback peer AND a real recording fake provider --
#   a_granted_sampling_ask_is_completed_on_the_operators_model_and_answered,
#   the_per_upstream_budget_refuses_the_completion_past_the_cap_before_it_runs,
#   an_ungranted_sampling_ask_is_still_refused_and_spends_nothing,
#   a_granted_ask_with_no_policy_refuses_and_names_the_key,
#   a_caller_whose_key_does_not_reach_the_pool_cannot_be_spent_through,
#   the_sampling_policy_is_refused_at_boot_when_it_cannot_mean_what_it_says;
# plus `mcp/tests/sampling_spend_tests.rs` on the budget in isolation. Watched red first: with the
# satisfier dispatch short-circuited to its old refusal the central case fails on "the satisfied
# exchange must complete" (403 `ask_unsatisfiable` where 200 is owed).
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|sampling/createMessage = implemented

# ── THE STREAMABLE-HTTP CLIENT COLUMN, ISSUED (1.6.0) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ONE verb enum and ONE governed send site serve BOTH client transports. `mcp/client/verb.rs`'s
# `UpstreamVerb` and `mcp/client/issue.rs`'s `issue` landed with the stdio leg; this transport adds
# no second copy of either, which is the point — `Transport::mcp_wire()` is the only place in the
# tree that asks the axis which variant it is, and from there down the leg is bytes out and bytes
# back identically for an HTTPS POST and a write to a child's stdin.
#
# WHAT THIS TRANSPORT ADDED, because a child process has no analogue for any of it:
#   * `upstream::authorise_verb` — the SERVER-SCOPED gate. `Authorised` now carries its own `server`
#     and an OPTIONAL `key`, because a verb names no tool. Before that the only constructor of the
#     value the send site needs resolved a TOOL, so issuing a `prompts/list` required an `mcp_tool`
#     grant while `egress::ServerVerb`, the subject it is actually gated on, correctly requires one.
#     A deployment fronting an upstream for its prompts and no tools could not reach it.
#   * The ordered validator on that path: identity, the `mcp_server` grant, whether the registration
#     is serving at all, then the snapshot generation. `capability: None` is a statement — a verb has
#     no per-capability fingerprint — and the REGISTRATION-level half still runs, so a quarantined
#     upstream serves no `prompts/list` either.
#   * `client::argguard` on the verb's OWN params, inside `issue`, for every verb however the leg was
#     authorised. A `resources/read` carries a caller-chosen URI, and the routing rule makes the
#     DESTINATION immune to attacker-chosen text and does nothing whatever for the PAYLOAD.
#   * The `Mcp-Name` rule unified with `mcp::ingress::name_source_of`. The builder had its own copy
#     and the two DISAGREED about the three tasks methods, so a `tasks/get` went out with no
#     `Mcp-Name` — the exact header busbar's own front door answers `-32020` to. Invisible on stdio,
#     which has no headers.
#
# EVIDENCE: `mcp/tests/http_client_leg_tests.rs`. THE DENOMINATOR IS DERIVED — it reads this column
# out of qa/method-inventory.json, subtracts the waivers out of this file, and fails if the set it
# drives is not equal in both directions. No count is written down anywhere in it. Every owed method
# is issued at a real peer on a real loopback socket and asserted at the RECEIVING end: the method,
# `id` present exactly when there is an answer to correlate, the three mirrored headers, the RFC 8693
# EXCHANGED bearer, and one per-call record each. Controls: an ungranted caller leaves the upstream's
# AND the authorization server's own hit counters at zero; a link-local URI in a verb's params is
# refused before the exchange; an unpinned registration issues nothing; an upstream's ask terminates
# and its text never reaches busbar's caller; a wildcard principal's exchange asks for no tool scope.
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|server/discover = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|prompts/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|prompts/get = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|resources/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|resources/read = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|resources/templates/list = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|completion/complete = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|tasks/get = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|tasks/update = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|tasks/cancel = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|subscriptions/listen = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|notifications/cancelled = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|notifications/progress = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|notifications/roots/list_changed = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|client|notifications/elicitation/response = implemented

# ── WHAT AN UPSTREAM SAYS BACK OVER STREAMABLE HTTP, HANDLED (1.6.0) ────────────────────────────
#
# SEP-2575 removed the standalone GET stream; it did not remove server-to-client messages. They ride
# the SSE response of a POST busbar made, and that response is this leg's whole inbound surface.
# Everything on it except `notifications/progress` was DROPPED by `last_sse_data`, and a dropped
# frame is not a handled frame: a `…/list_changed` nobody saw is a rug-pull signal nobody acted on.
#
# THE CLASSIFIER IS `mcp/client/peer.rs`'s, shared with the stdio leg, which reads the same messages
# off a child's stdout. Two carriers, one meaning — `peer::classify` decides what a message IS and
# `ServerNotification::effect` decides what busbar DOES, and this transport supplies only the SSE
# reader and the effect application. A second table here would be a second answer to "may this
# notification move busbar's catalogue", and the copy that got fixed would not be the one that was
# wrong. The four catalogue triggers bring a re-pull FORWARD through the `RefreshGate` already on the
# pool; the notification's CONTENTS are never read.
#
# THE ONE GENUINE CARRIER DIFFERENCE, and it is why `ping` is claimed here rather than waived: stdio
# ANSWERS a peer's request, because a child's stdin is a channel and a child left waiting hangs. An
# SSE response body is not a channel — it is the answer to a POST already sent — so a request
# arriving here CANNOT be answered. It is recognised, recorded and dropped, and never adopted as the
# answer to what busbar actually asked. Over this carrier the three authority asks are refused by the
# absence of any way to satisfy them, which is stronger than the grant gate: busbar cannot say yes
# even where an operator granted it.
#
# EVIDENCE: `mcp/client/tests/http_peer_tests.rs`. All nine notifications, all four peer requests and
# two unrecognised frames arrive on ONE real SSE stream from a real loopback socket. The returned
# body is asserted to be the POST's own answer, to carry no `method`, and to contain none of a
# notification's attacker-chosen payload; the progress channel is asserted to have received exactly
# one frame; a flood of twenty-five `…/list_changed` brings exactly one re-pull forward and a second
# flood inside the floor brings none; and every notification is asserted to move the catalogue
# exactly when `peer`'s own effect table says it does. All three authority grants are GRANTED on the
# leg under test, so the request refusals are not the grant gate answering.
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/message = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/cancelled = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/prompts/list_changed = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/resources/list_changed = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/resources/updated = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/subscriptions/acknowledged = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/tasks = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/tools/list_changed = implemented
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|ping = implemented

# The upstream's progress, relayed to busbar's own caller. ONLY that method is taken off the
# upstream's stream — a stream may carry anything, and relaying whatever happened to be on it would
# put an upstream's messages in front of busbar's caller under busbar's name. The frames are
# re-tokened on the way out: busbar mints its own progress token for the upstream leg, so relaying
# the upstream's spelling would be uncorrelatable to the caller and relaying busbar's would leak an
# internal identifier. Instrument: the official suite's `tools-call-with-progress`, REQUIRED at this
# revision, which drives an upstream that reports progress and reads what reached the caller.
mcp|streamable-http|client|server|notifications/progress = implemented

# ── A2A, busbar as SERVER, over gRPC ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A2A over gRPC, busbar answering. Ten of the service's eleven rpcs, each watched green on the
# publisher's own instrument: `scripts/a2a-subject/boot.sh --tck` against a busbar built from this
# commit, TCK pinned at 5996b79f9cefa6fc390980e383e358a66fb9e49e. The suite's own per-transport
# table read `grpc: 0/72 (72 skipped)` before this leg armed and reads `grpc: 62/72 (4 skipped)`
# after it, and the requirement behind each line below is recorded beside it.
#
# GetExtendedAgentCard IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT from this block, and the reason CHANGED IN THE MERGE
# rather than survived it. On the branch that armed this binding the eleventh rpc answered
# UNIMPLEMENTED, because busbar served no extended agent card at all. It serves one now -- the
# JSON-RPC cell above is this release's, landed on the other branch -- and this rpc is wired to the
# same single ingress as the other ten, so the merged tree really does answer it here.
#
# It stays MISSING because NO INSTRUMENT HAS WATCHED IT DO SO. The gRPC leg was scored before the
# extended card existed, and the suite records CARD-EXT-001/002 as SKIPPED on every transport, so
# there is no run to read. That is exactly the bar the header sets: a cell is claimed when its leg
# has been watched green, not when the code that should make it green is in the tree.
a2a|grpc|server|client|SendMessage = implemented                      # CORE-SEND-001/002/003
a2a|grpc|server|client|SendStreamingMessage = implemented             # STREAM-ORDER-001..004
a2a|grpc|server|client|GetTask = implemented                          # CORE-GET-001/002
a2a|grpc|server|client|ListTasks = implemented                        # CORE-LIST-001..005
a2a|grpc|server|client|CancelTask = implemented                       # CORE-CANCEL-001/002/003
a2a|grpc|server|client|SubscribeToTask = implemented                  # STREAM-SUB-001..004
a2a|grpc|server|client|CreateTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented  # PUSH-CREATE-001/002
a2a|grpc|server|client|GetTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented     # PUSH-GET-001/002
a2a|grpc|server|client|ListTaskPushNotificationConfigs = implemented   # PUSH-LIST-001
a2a|grpc|server|client|DeleteTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented  # PUSH-DEL-001/002
# MCP CLIENT DIRECTION, STDIO TRANSPORT. An authenticated inbound `tools/call` on
# busbar's own front door reaches a SUPERVISED CHILD PROCESS: spawn, newline-delimited
# JSON-RPC on its stdin/stdout, capped backoff on a crash and a five-crashes-in-a-window
# quarantine. Watched green on the instrument, not inferred:
#   mcp::upstream::stdio_dispatch_tests::a_tools_call_reaches_a_child_process_and_returns_its_result
#   mcp::upstream::stdio_dispatch_tests::a_crash_looping_child_backs_off_and_is_quarantined_through_the_dispatch_path
# The second one goes RED if the supervisor is bypassed, which is what makes the first
# one a claim about a transport rather than about a fork.
mcp|stdio|client|client|tools/call = implemented

# MCP CLIENT DIRECTION, STDIO TRANSPORT, `tools/list`. The drift re-check asks a CHILD PROCESS for
# its tool list over the same vtable the call rides, so an operator's approved digests are compared
# against what the child is actually serving. Watched green on the instrument:
#   mcp::upstream::stdio_dispatch_tests::a_refresh_re_observes_a_child_process_tool_list
# It is claimed rather than left in the queue because the alternative was worse than absent: the
# refresh used to send through the HTTP transport unconditionally, which on a stdio registration
# would have POSTed to an empty string and DEMOTED a healthy server for failing a contact busbar
# addressed to nowhere.
mcp|stdio|client|client|tools/list = implemented

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# waived
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# EMPTY, BY RULING, AND KEPT EMPTY BY THE GATE. Owner, 2026-08-14: "100%, and the waiver list
# should stay empty." The 63 waivers this section carried on 2026-08-12 went three ways, and none
# of the three is a line here:
#
#   * PRODUCT DECISIONS went back to the WORK QUEUE. A waiver whose own text says "a product
#     decision, not a technical block. Reversible." is not a waiver -- it is owed work wearing a
#     waiver's clothes. The 36-cell mcp|stdio SERVER family (busbar itself launched as a child
#     process), the upstream-directed `sampling/createMessage` satisfier ("design work rather than
#     plumbing"), and `notifications/resources/updated` (a capability narrowing) are pinned in
#     qa/method-coverage.missing, which is and remains the 1.6.0 work queue.
#   * MISSING POLICIES got their policy and got BUILT. `roots/list` asked by an upstream is
#     satisfied from operator-declared `tools.<server>.roots`, and a caller's
#     `notifications/roots/list_changed` invalidates the caller's own outstanding roots-bearing
#     `requestState`. Both are claimed `implemented` above, each on an instrument, each watched
#     red before green.
#   * GENUINE IMPOSSIBILITIES are RECORDED IN qa/WAIVERS.md, one paragraph of argument each: the
#     23 cells where the specification removed the method and the official suite asserts the
#     absence (16), where the revision provides no carrier a conformant message could ride (5), or
#     where satisfying the ask is itself the defect a security property exists to prevent (2).
#     `crates/busbar/tests/method_coverage.rs::recorded_impossibilities_are_exact_and_argued`
#     holds that file to the same discipline this one is held to -- exact ids, no globs, no
#     staleness, no overlap with a claim or with the queue, and a real argument -- and an
#     impossibility whose argument stops holding moves to the queue, not back here.

a2a|grpc|client|client|CancelTask = implemented                         # grpc_client_issues_cancel_task
a2a|grpc|client|client|CreateTaskPushNotificationConfig = implemented   # grpc_client_issues_create_task_push_notification_config
